1:# SPEC: Phase 5 — Messaging Resilience
5:Status: Final v4 — revised after adversarial reviews r2–r4 (`reviews/sol-review-spec-r2.md` … `r4.md`); r4 fixes 
applied without a fifth review by owner decision D7 (2026-10-06). Approved by the owner 2026-10-06 (native question 
UI: "Aprovada"). Interview rounds 1–2, confirmation, and owner decisions D1–D6 recorded in `state.md` (Verdict: Ready).
7:## 1. Summary
9:The Virtual Office messaging drawer (`MessagingDrawer` → `MessageFeed` → `MessageComposer` / `MessageItem`, state in 
`MessagingContext`, data in `useMessages` / `useConversations`, Realtime in `useMessageSubscription` / 
`useConversationRealtime`, typing in `useConversationPresence`) was completed for timeline features in Phase 4. Phase 
4 also delivered a failed send that keeps text/reply/files with a single-message retry (`clientMessageId` idempotency 
in `POST /api/messages/create`, stored in `messages.client_message_id`), a Realtime catch-up after reconnect, and 
opt-in desktop notifications for DM/group messages. This phase adds: a durable per-user outbox for text messages with 
server-fenced attempt generations and safe cross-tab and cross-account behavior; a connection banner; 
application-level recovery and health for every messaging Realtime stream with polling fallback; a reliable, named, 
members-only typing indicator; live cross-device unread/read sync; @mentions with desktop notifications (including 
rooms); messaging health metrics as redacted server logs; and two Phase 4 database follow-ups plus restoring the open 
conversation after reload.
11:## 2. Problem statement
13:Workspace members using the chat on unstable networks or sleeping laptops lose work and trust: a send that fails 
must be retried by hand, a reload or closed tab discards it (the idempotency key lives only in component memory), and 
nothing tells the user the chat is disconnected. Optimistic messages are matched to stored ones by guessing from 
content and time. Realtime recovery is incomplete: the SDK retries errored/timed-out channels and the 
conversation-list stream invalidates the list after its own rejoins, but the application exposes health only for the 
messages channel, does not recover terminal closures of the conversation-list channel, has no aggregate health, and 
has no polling fallback, so new messages and unread counts can silently stop updating. The typing indicator disappears 
after ~4 s of continuous typing and is broadcast on a public channel. Reading on one device leaves stale unread badges 
on the user's other devices. Nobody can mention someone, so room conversations (which never notify) cannot reach a 
specific person. Nobody can tell in production whether messaging is healthy.
15:## 3. Goals and success metrics
17:### Goals
18:- G1: A text message the user sends is never silently lost: it is delivered, pending, or visibly failed with an 
action.
27:### Success metrics
28:- With ≤ 10 pending messages and create quota available, all pending messages are delivered within 10 s after 
connectivity returns, without any click, in their original order, each exactly once; with more, delivery continues 
automatically within the server rate limit (D1).
36:## 4. Users and stakeholders
38:### Primary users
41:### Secondary users
45:### Stakeholders
48:## 5. Scope
50:### In scope
51:- Durable outbox (IndexedDB, per application user, per browser) for text messages and replies, written before 
dispatch; item states; cancel; per-conversation ordered automatic drain across tabs; claim leases and recovery; 
server-fenced attempts; identity fencing; rejection, rate-limit, and 24 h expiry handling; session-end and sign-out 
rules; sending blocked with a clear message when durable storage is unavailable (D5).
62:### Out of scope
65:- Editing a pending message (cancel and rewrite instead).
72:- Other Phase 4 follow-ups: pending-upload cap, toaster covering the composer, pin no-op/English copy, orphan 
storage objects, "Favoritas" discoverability.
75:## 6. User journeys
77:- J1 Offline send: A loses internet and keeps writing; each message appears as pending; the banner says "Sem 
conexão — mensagens serão enviadas quando voltar". Internet returns; with ≤ 10 pending and quota available, every 
pending message is delivered within 10 s in order and B sees each once; with more, delivery continues within the rate 
limit (D1).
78:- J2 Reload/close: A has pending messages and reloads while offline (the browser shows its normal offline error) or 
closes the tab. When the page loads again with internet, the pending messages are there and are sent.
79:- J3 Two tabs: A's pending message shows in both tabs as one item; only one tab sends a conversation's messages at 
a time; if that tab closes or hangs mid-send, the other finishes them once; cancelling a waiting message in either tab 
removes it from both.
80:- J4 Rejection: A's pending message targets a room A has since lost access to; it becomes a visible failure with 
the text kept and "Tentar de novo" / "Apagar"; later pending messages for that room wait; other conversations still 
deliver.
81:- J5 Sign-out: A clicks Sair with 2 unsent messages; a dialog warns that leaving deletes them; Cancelar leaves A 
exactly where A was; Sair mesmo signs A out.
89:## 7. Functional requirements
93:| FR-001 | Every eligible send (text and/or reply, no attachments/voice; online or offline) is first written 
durably to the current user's outbox with its `clientMessageId`, conversation, content, reply target, and requested 
mention IDs, and only then dispatched and the composer cleared. If the durable write fails, nothing is dispatched 
(FR-033). The item shows in the feed as pending while not confirmed by the server. | Must | AC-001, AC-003, AC-040 |
94:| FR-002 | Pending items are sent automatically when connectivity returns (browser online, Realtime recovery, or 
periodic retry), per conversation in creation order, always with their own `clientMessageId`. With ≤ 10 pending and 
quota available, all are delivered within 10 s of connectivity returning; with more, delivery continues within the 
rate limit. A confirmed item becomes the normal sent message and leaves the outbox. | Must | AC-002, AC-009, AC-038 |
95:| FR-003 | The outbox survives reload and tab close. Reloading while offline shows the browser's normal offline 
error; when the app loads again, the pending items are shown and sent. Items of the current user appear in every tab 
of the same browser. | Must | AC-004 |
96:| FR-004 | Cross-tab ordering and ownership: the outbox order is shared by all tabs; per conversation, only the 
oldest unresolved item is eligible, and at most one tab holds the conversation's drain claim at a time (atomic claim). 
A claim is a lease: it ends when its tab closes or crashes, or when its holder exceeds the attempt deadline (request 
timeout 30 s; lease 45 s), and another tab then resumes after fencing (FR-006). When connectivity returns, an attempt 
that started before or during the outage is superseded immediately (fenced and redispatched under a new generation by 
whichever tab drains), so a hung holder never delays the D1 deadline. Across all tabs, no two create requests of the 
same conversation's outbox are in progress at once. | Must | AC-005, AC-041 |
99:| FR-007 | A definitive rejection (BR-005) stops automatic attempts for that item; it shows as failed with its text 
and actions "Tentar de novo" and "Apagar"; later items of the same conversation wait until it is resolved; other 
conversations are unaffected. For `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, "Tentar de novo" sends the same content under a new 
`clientMessageId`. | Must | AC-007, AC-008, AC-043, AC-046 |
100:| FR-008 | A rate-limit rejection is transient: the item stays pending and is retried automatically, preserving 
order and without duplicates. The insertion quota is charged only for a winning insertion (including among concurrent 
requests for the same key); replays that return an already-stored message do not consume it (D1). Independently, every 
create request (including replays, validation failures, and quota losers) consumes a per-user request budget, and 
fence/lookup calls consume their own per-user budget; exceeding any budget is a transient rate limit. | Must | AC-009 |
102:| FR-010 | Attachments/voice are never queued. A file whose upload fails offline keeps the existing per-file error 
and retry (send stays disabled until uploads finish); a message whose uploads succeeded but whose create request fails 
keeps the Phase 4 composer failure and "Tentar de novo". | Must | AC-011 |
105:| FR-013 | When the user clicks Sair at any in-app sign-out entry point with N > 0 unsent items, a dialog shows 
"Você tem N mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens." with "Sair mesmo" and "Cancelar". Cancelar produces 
no logout request, success notice, or navigation. Sair mesmo stops drains in all tabs, deletes that user's outbox, and 
runs the existing sign-out (including the Presence logout sequence unchanged). With N = 0 sign-out is unchanged. | 
Must | AC-013 |
117:| FR-025 | With the Phase 4 desktop-notification toggle on and permission granted, a mentioned user receives one 
desktop notification for a message that mentions them (direct, group, room, thread reply), titled "<Autor> mencionou 
você em <conversa>" with the full text; clicking opens the conversation. Before emitting, the app confirms with 
authoritative data, under the current identity, that the user is still a member and (for rooms) mentioned; then, with 
no further awaits before display, it re-checks that the same user is still signed in and that no removal signal for 
that conversation has arrived; otherwise the pending notification is discarded, as it is on any account switch or 
sign-out. Residual race accepted (D6): a removal that the app has not yet received when the final check runs may let 
that one notification show. Suppression is browser-wide: no tab notifies if any of the user's tabs in that browser is 
visible and viewing that conversation, regardless of which tab receives the event first. | Must | AC-025, AC-026, 
AC-049 |
119:| FR-027 | The client records metric events — send latency (press → server confirmed), outbox wait time, send 
outcomes by reason, Realtime stream drops/recoveries and outage duration, polling activations and duration — in a 
bounded buffer (oldest dropped when full) and sends them fire-and-forget in batches of at most 50 events and 16 KB, at 
most one delivery in flight, each aborted after 5 s, dropping batches under overload. The authenticated, per-user 
rate-limited route rejects oversized requests, accepts only closed event/reason enums and bounded numeric fields, 
drops anything else, adds a server-derived app version, and writes structured log lines under BR-013. | Must | AC-028 |
125:| FR-033 | When the outbox cannot be written (site storage unavailable or the write fails), nothing is dispatched; 
the text and reply target stay in the composer with the message "Este navegador não permite guardar mensagens; libere 
o armazenamento do site" (D5). | Must | AC-039 |
126:| FR-034 | Key-based correlation: the sender's representations of a message (outbox item, create and fence 
responses, feed fetches, Realtime events) carry its `clientMessageId`, and the feed matches pending items to stored 
messages only by that key, so each message shows as exactly one bubble in every sender tab, including identical texts 
and Realtime arriving before the HTTP response. | Must | AC-050 |
128:## 8. Business rules
132:| BR-001 | The outbox belongs to one application user (`users.id`) on one browser; it is never shown, dispatched, 
fenced, or deleted while another user is authenticated, and the server never stores or fences an item under a sender 
other than its owner. | R1-q3 A, R1-q16 A, R2-q5 A; reviews r2 #6, r3 #4 |
133:| BR-002 | Only text messages and replies without attachments/voice are queued. | R1-q4 A |
134:| BR-003 | Per conversation, items are sent in creation order with at most one create request in progress across 
all tabs; a failed (definitive or expired) item blocks later items of that conversation until the user retries or 
deletes it. Conversations are independent. | R1-q5 A, R1-q2 E; review r3 #5 |
136:| BR-005 | Outcome classes: transient = browser offline before dispatch, HTTP 429, HTTP 401 while the client 
session is valid → retry automatically; unknown = network error/timeout/no response after dispatch, lease expiry, HTTP 
5xx → fence (FR-006); session-ended = client auth reports signed out / refresh rejected → hold (FR-012); definitive = 
403 `NOT_PARTICIPANT` (incl. a missing conversation), 400 (e.g. `INVALID_REPLY_TARGET`), 413 `CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`, 409 
`CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, sender mismatch, and any other 4xx except 401/429 → failed, no automatic retry; a 
stale-generation rejection only means a newer attempt exists and is ignored (FR-011). | R1-q5 C, R2-q4 A, R2-q5 A; 
reviews r2 #2, #11 |
139:| BR-008 | An explicit user sign-out with unsent items requires confirmation; confirming deletes that user's 
outbox; a sign-out not initiated by the user keeps it. Cancelling has no side effect. | R2-q5 A, R2-q6 A |
149:## 9. Data and integrations
151:### Data inputs
156:### Data outputs
157:- Browser IndexedDB outbox keyed by application user ID: `clientMessageId`, conversation ID, text, reply target, 
requested mention user IDs, pressed-send time, delivery-window start, order, state (waiting / claimed / unknown / 
failed + reason / expired), attempt generation, claim holder and lease expiry. Deleted per BR-007/BR-008; never shared 
across users.
163:### Integrations
168:## 10. Constraints and assumptions
170:### Constraints
181:### Assumptions
184:## 11. Edge cases and failure modes
188:| Tab closed right after pressing send, before server confirmation | Item is already in the outbox; on next load 
it is fenced and delivered once (AC-040). |
192:| Hung holder when connectivity returns | Its attempt is superseded at once; D1 deadline holds (AC-041). |
200:| Server rate limit during drain; concurrent same-key requests | Items stay pending, retried automatically; quota 
charged once per stored message; replays/fences use a separate budget (AC-009). |
202:| Offline attachment / voice | Upload failure: per-file error + retry; create failure after upload: Phase 4 
composer failure; nothing in the outbox (AC-011). |
203:| IndexedDB unavailable or write fails | Nothing dispatched; text stays in the composer with the storage message 
(D5) (AC-039). |
208:| Sair with unsent items | Dialog; Cancelar has no side effects at any entry point; Sair mesmo stops drains 
everywhere and deletes the outbox; late responses do not resurrect items (AC-013, AC-047). |
223:## 12. Security, privacy, compliance, and abuse considerations
226:- Every create request consumes a per-user request budget and fence/lookup calls their own, so the insertion-quota 
exemption for replays (D1) cannot be used for unlimited traffic.
235:## 13. Accessibility, localization, and usability considerations
236:- UI copy in Brazilian Portuguese. The banner text, sign-out dialog text, typing phrases, and mention notification 
title in this SPEC are required; item-state labels ("pendente", "enviando", "expirada"), actions ("Cancelar", "Tentar 
de novo", "Apagar"), are proposed (the storage message in FR-033 is the owner's D5 wording), and equivalent Portuguese 
wording with the same meaning is accepted (D4).
242:## 14. Acceptance criteria
244:- AC-001 (FR-001, BR-002): Given A's browser context is offline, when A sends a text message, then A's feed shows 
it as pending with a text label, the composer is empty, the outbox holds it, and B does not see it.
245:- AC-002 (FR-002, BR-003, BR-004): Given A queued 3 messages offline in conversation X with quota available, when 
connectivity returns, then within 10 s and without any click B sees exactly those 3 messages, once each, in the 
original order, and A's items become normal sent messages.
246:- AC-003 (FR-001, FR-006, BR-005): Given the browser reports online but A's create request fails with a network 
error after dispatch, then the item becomes unknown, is fenced, and is delivered exactly once without user action once 
requests succeed.
247:- AC-004 (FR-003): Given A has a pending item, when A reloads while offline, then the browser's offline error is 
shown; when the network returns and the app loads again, the item is shown as pending and is delivered exactly once. 
Given A closes the tab with a pending item, then reopening the app later delivers it once.
248:- AC-005 (FR-004, BR-003, BR-004; must-not R1-q16 B): Given A has two tabs and queued m1, m2 offline in 
conversation X in tab 1, then both appear once in tab 2; when both tabs come back online simultaneously and m1's real 
create request is held, then neither tab sends a create request for m2 until m1 is resolved; the network logs of both 
tabs show no overlapping create requests for X; B receives m1 then m2, exactly once each; the database holds one row 
per key.
250:- AC-007 (FR-007, BR-003, BR-005): Given A queued m1 and m2 in room conversation X and m3 in DM Y, and A lost 
access to X before reconnecting, when connectivity returns, then m1 shows failed with its text and "Tentar de novo" / 
"Apagar", m2 stays waiting with no create request sent, and m3 is delivered.
251:- AC-008 (FR-007, BR-003): Given AC-007's state, when A regains access to X and clicks "Tentar de novo" on m1, 
then m1 and then m2 are delivered in order; given instead A clicks "Apagar" on m1, then m1 is never delivered and m2 
is attempted next.
252:- AC-009 (FR-002, FR-008, BR-005): Given A's create quota is exhausted when A's 12 queued items start draining, 
then rejected items stay pending and retry automatically, and B finally sees all 12 in order, once each. On the real 
route: a replay of an already-stored message does not reduce the insertion quota; replays, validation failures, and 
quota losers all consume the request budget and are rejected (429) once it is exhausted; fence/lookup calls are 
limited by their own budget; concurrent requests for one new key charge the insertion quota once; concurrent distinct 
keys at the quota boundary store no more messages than the quota allows.
254:- AC-011 (FR-010, BR-002): Given A is offline, when A adds a file, then its upload shows the existing per-file 
error and retry, Send stays disabled, and nothing enters the outbox; given A's uploads succeeded and then the network 
drops, when A sends, then the message stays in the composer with the Phase 4 failure and "Tentar de novo", and nothing 
enters the outbox.
255:- AC-012 (FR-011, FR-012, BR-001, BR-005; must-not R1-q16 A): Given A has unsent items and A's browser session 
ends without Sair (auth cookies/storage cleared or refresh token revoked so the client reports signed out), when C 
signs in on the same browser, then C sees none of A's items and no create or fence request is sent while C is 
authenticated; when A signs in again, A's items (< 24 h) are delivered as A. Given a 401 while A's client session is 
valid, then the item stays pending and is retried.
256:- AC-013 (FR-013, BR-008): Given A has 2 unsent items, when A clicks Sair from each in-app entry point, then the 
dialog shows "Você tem 2 mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens."; Cancelar sends no logout request, shows 
no notice, and does not navigate; Sair mesmo with a drain in flight in another tab stops all drains, deletes the 
outbox, and signs out with the Presence logout sequence as before; after A signs in again the outbox is empty. Given 0 
unsent items, Sair signs out without a dialog.
267:- AC-024 (FR-023, FR-024, BR-010; must-not R1-q16 F): On the real route and local DB: a message mentioning a 
non-member ID and the sender's own ID stores no mention for either and notifies neither; a replay that changes only 
the requested mention IDs returns `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED` and changes nothing; a message whose text contains 
"@Bruno" typed without selection stores no mention; a forced failure of mention storage stores neither the message nor 
any mention; anon and non-members cannot read mention records; no client can insert/update/delete them directly.
268:- AC-025 (FR-025, BR-011): Given B has notifications enabled and permission granted, B's tab hidden or viewing 
another conversation, when A mentions B in a room, then B receives exactly one notification "<A> mencionou você em 
<sala>" with the full text; clicking opens that room in the drawer; a room message without a mention of B produces 
none.
269:- AC-026 (FR-025, BR-011; must-not R1-q16 C): Given B has notifications enabled, then a DM mention yields exactly 
