90: -- reads the newer generation and rolls back. Conversely, a create that already
91: -- holds the key row lock keeps it until commit, so a concurrent fence waits
92: -- and then (new READ COMMITTED snapshot per statement) sees the committed
93: -- message. Fencing a tracked key never waits on message rows (repairing an
94: -- untracked delivery may wait on its message row through the FK check). Either
95: -- way at most one message
